<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    jumptablemax: a guard-bounded table longer than the cap is read to its
    guard's bound only when each table slot it reads stands for at most four
    selector values.

    Source (gcc 11 -O2 -fPIC, x86-64):
      int wide(unsigned int x) {
        switch (x >> 8) { case 0: return 11; case 3: return x * 3 + 1; ...
                          case 40: return x + 400; }
        return 7;
      }
    gcc guards the original value (`cmp $0x28ff,%edi; ja`) and indexes a
    41-entry table of 32-bit displacements with `x >> 8`, so the guard admits
    0x2900 values of x over 41 slots.

    Fixed: the table stays a computed call (`// jump-as-call` behind
    `if (x <= 0x28ff)`), as with every table past jumptablemax before the
    guard bound.
    Before: a switch on x with 2560 case labels, one per value of x.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1100" readonly="true">

</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x2000" readonly="true">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1100" name="wide"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern int4 wide(uint4 x);</com>
  <com>lo fu wide</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="JUMPTABLESLOTS #1: the 41-slot table indexed by x &gt;&gt; 8 stays a computed call" min="1" max="1">// jump-as-call</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="JUMPTABLESLOTS #2: no value of x prints as its own case label" min="0" max="0">case </stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="JUMPTABLESLOTS #3: the guard on x still prints" min="1" max="1">x &lt;= 0x28ff</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
